-- ECAT and NUST NET English: 91 original MCQs in the style of the SAT, NEN-0001 to NEN-0091.
-- Written for Markhor Prep, not copied from any book or other bank: ten original reading passages with five questions
-- each (main idea, detail, inference, meaning from context, purpose, structure, evidence, tone), and sentence
-- completions with one or two blanks.
--
-- The MDCAT English set has few passage questions, so these fill that gap for the engineering tests. They are English
-- questions under existing chapters, with questions.mdcat = 0 so they stay out of the MDCAT bank and mocks, and are
-- listed in the ECAT and NET banks (lib/banks.ts). Not in the free set. INSERT OR IGNORE keeps the file safe to run
-- twice; without the site owner's account (a fresh local database) nothing is inserted.

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, mdcat, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'English', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('NEN-0001', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Main idea', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Read the passage and answer the question.
For centuries, farmers along the Indus relied on the river''s yearly flood to water and enrich their fields. The silt it left behind was a free fertiliser, and planting followed the rhythm of the water. Canals and barrages changed that rhythm. They allowed water to be stored and released when crops needed it, and millions of acres that had been desert came under the plough. Yet the same control brought new troubles. Water that seeps from unlined canals raises the water table, and in hot weather it evaporates, leaving salt on the surface. Land that was made fertile by irrigation can, after a few decades, become too salty to farm. Engineers now argue that the next great gain will come not from building more canals but from wasting less of the water already flowing through them.

Which statement best expresses the main idea of the passage?', '["The yearly flood of the Indus was better for farmers than canals.", "Engineers should build more canals and barrages.", "Salt on the surface of the soil is caused by hot weather.", "Irrigation turned desert into farmland but also created problems that now demand better use of water."]', 3, 'The passage moves from the gains of canals to their drawbacks (waterlogging and salt) and ends with the call to waste less water.', '{"0": "The passage praises both systems in different ways; it does not rank the flood above canals.", "1": "The last sentence says the opposite: the gain lies in wasting less, not building more.", "2": "That is one detail, and heat alone is not the cause; seepage is."}'),
  ('NEN-0002', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Detail', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Read the passage and answer the question.
