Why does the author mention salt left on the surface?', '["To explain how silt fertilises the soil", "To praise the work of engineers", "To show a harmful side effect of canal irrigation", "To describe the climate of the Indus valley"]', 2, 'The salt is introduced with ''Yet the same control brought new troubles'', marking it as a drawback of canals.', '{"0": "Silt is a separate point, about the old floods.", "1": "The salt is a problem, not a success.", "3": "Hot weather is mentioned only as the cause of evaporation."}'),
  ('NEN-0006', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Main idea', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Read the passage and answer the question.
When a new medicine is tested, half the volunteers may receive the real drug and half a ''placebo'', a pill that looks the same but contains nothing active. Surprisingly, many people who take the placebo report feeling better. Researchers once dismissed this as imagination, but brain scans show that expecting relief can release the body''s own pain-reducing chemicals. This does not mean that placebos cure disease; they rarely shrink a tumour or kill bacteria. It does mean that a patient''s beliefs can change how symptoms are felt. For this reason, a fair trial must hide from both patients and doctors who is receiving which pill. Otherwise, hope on one side and disappointment on the other could make a useless drug look effective.

The passage is mainly about:', '["how expectations affect patients and why drug trials must be blind", "how placebos cure serious diseases", "the chemicals in new medicines", "why doctors dislike drug trials"]', 0, 'It explains the placebo effect and then why trials hide who gets which pill.', '{"1": "The passage says placebos rarely cure disease.", "2": "Placebos contain nothing active; the chemistry of drugs is not discussed.", "3": "Nothing suggests doctors dislike trials."}'),
  ('NEN-0007', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Detail', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Read the passage and answer the question.
When a new medicine is tested, half the volunteers may receive the real drug and half a ''placebo'', a pill that looks the same but contains nothing active. Surprisingly, many people who take the placebo report feeling better. Researchers once dismissed this as imagination, but brain scans show that expecting relief can release the body''s own pain-reducing chemicals. This does not mean that placebos cure disease; they rarely shrink a tumour or kill bacteria. It does mean that a patient''s beliefs can change how symptoms are felt. For this reason, a fair trial must hide from both patients and doctors who is receiving which pill. Otherwise, hope on one side and disappointment on the other could make a useless drug look effective.

What is a placebo, according to the passage?', '["A stronger version of the drug being tested", "A brain scan used in drug trials", "A chemical the body makes to reduce pain", "A pill that looks like the real drug but has nothing active in it"]', 3, 'The passage defines it as a pill that looks the same but contains nothing active.', '{"0": "It contains nothing active at all.", "1": "Brain scans are a research tool, not the placebo.", "2": "That describes the body''s own pain-reducing chemicals."}'),
  ('NEN-0008', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Inference', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Read the passage and answer the question.
When a new medicine is tested, half the volunteers may receive the real drug and half a ''placebo'', a pill that looks the same but contains nothing active. Surprisingly, many people who take the placebo report feeling better. Researchers once dismissed this as imagination, but brain scans show that expecting relief can release the body''s own pain-reducing chemicals. This does not mean that placebos cure disease; they rarely shrink a tumour or kill bacteria. It does mean that a patient''s beliefs can change how symptoms are felt. For this reason, a fair trial must hide from both patients and doctors who is receiving which pill. Otherwise, hope on one side and disappointment on the other could make a useless drug look effective.

Why must doctors, and not only patients, be kept unaware of who receives the real drug?', '["Doctors are not allowed to prescribe placebos.", "Their hopes or doubts could influence patients and the results.", "Doctors cannot tell the two pills apart anyway.", "It makes the trial shorter."]', 1, 'The passage warns that ''hope on one side and disappointment on the other'' could make a useless drug look effective; a doctor who knows can pass on that hope.', '{"0": "Nothing in the passage forbids it.", "2": "The pills look the same, but that is why hiding is possible, not why it is needed.", "3": "Length of the trial is not mentioned."}'),
  ('NEN-0009', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Meaning from context', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Read the passage and answer the question.
