In the passage, ''dismissed'' most nearly means:', '["sent away", "explained carefully", "rejected as unimportant", "proved"]', 2, 'Researchers once rejected the effect as mere imagination.', '{"0": "That is the literal sense, as of a class or worker.", "1": "They did the opposite of explaining it.", "3": "Dismissing an idea is not proving it."}'),
  ('NEN-0010', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Author''s attitude', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Read the passage and answer the question.
When a new medicine is tested, half the volunteers may receive the real drug and half a ''placebo'', a pill that looks the same but contains nothing active. Surprisingly, many people who take the placebo report feeling better. Researchers once dismissed this as imagination, but brain scans show that expecting relief can release the body''s own pain-reducing chemicals. This does not mean that placebos cure disease; they rarely shrink a tumour or kill bacteria. It does mean that a patient''s beliefs can change how symptoms are felt. For this reason, a fair trial must hide from both patients and doctors who is receiving which pill. Otherwise, hope on one side and disappointment on the other could make a useless drug look effective.

The author''s attitude towards the placebo effect is best described as:', '["balanced: it is real but limited", "dismissive", "enthusiastic: it can replace medicine", "fearful"]', 0, 'The author says it really changes how symptoms are felt, but that placebos rarely cure disease.', '{"1": "The author treats it as real, backed by brain scans.", "2": "The author says placebos rarely shrink a tumour or kill bacteria.", "3": "Nothing in the tone suggests fear."}'),
  ('NEN-0011', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Detail', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Read the passage and answer the question.
The first bicycles had no pedals at all: riders pushed themselves along with their feet, like children on a balance bike. Pedals attached to the front wheel came next, and to go faster makers simply enlarged that wheel until some stood taller than a man. These ''high-wheelers'' were quick but dangerous; a stone in the road could throw the rider head-first. The real breakthrough was the chain drive, which linked the pedals to the rear wheel through gears. Speed no longer depended on wheel size, so both wheels could be small and equal. Add air-filled tyres, which smoothed out rough roads, and by the 1890s the ''safety bicycle'' had taken a form that has hardly changed since.

What did the earliest bicycles lack?', '["Wheels", "A seat", "Handlebars", "Pedals"]', 3, 'The first sentence says they ''had no pedals at all''.', '{"0": "They had wheels; riders pushed along with their feet.", "1": "The passage does not say they lacked a seat.", "2": "Handlebars are not mentioned."}'),
  ('NEN-0012', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Detail', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Read the passage and answer the question.
