FROM (VALUES
  ('NEN-0021', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Main idea', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Read the passage and answer the question.
Many people believe that we use only ten per cent of our brains. The idea is appealing, since it suggests that great powers lie unused inside us, but it is false. Scans show that even simple tasks activate areas across the whole brain, and over a day almost every region is busy. The brain is also expensive to run: it is about two per cent of body weight but uses about a fifth of the body''s energy. Evolution would hardly have produced such a costly organ if most of it sat idle. Damage to almost any part of the brain, however small, causes some loss of ability. The myth survives not because of evidence but because it flatters us.

The author''s main purpose is to:', '["show that a popular belief about the brain is false", "explain how to use more of the brain", "describe how brain scans work", "argue that the brain uses too much energy"]', 0, 'The passage states the ten-per-cent belief and gives evidence against it.', '{"1": "The passage says nearly all of it is already used.", "2": "Scans are evidence, not the subject.", "3": "The energy figure supports the argument; it is not the argument."}'),
  ('NEN-0022', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Evidence', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Read the passage and answer the question.
