Along the coast of Sindh, where the Indus meets the sea, stand forests whose trees grow in salt water. These mangroves have roots that arch above the mud, and among them young fish and shrimp shelter until they are large enough for the open sea. The roots also trap sediment and slow the waves, so a belt of mangroves can shield a village from the worst of a storm surge. For a long time such forests were seen as useless swamps, to be cut for firewood or cleared for ports. Today they are valued more highly, yet they face a quieter threat. As more of the river''s water is taken upstream for farms and cities, less fresh water reaches the delta, and the sea creeps inland. Planting new trees helps, but without enough river water the forests cannot thrive.

