  ('NEN-0031', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Main idea', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Read the passage and answer the question.
The symbol for zero seems so obvious to us that it is hard to imagine arithmetic without it. Yet for most of history people managed without one. Roman numerals, for example, have no zero, and a long multiplication written in them is a slow and clumsy task. Indian mathematicians not only used a dot or circle to mark an empty place in a number but also, by the seventh century, wrote rules for adding and multiplying with zero as a number in its own right. Scholars writing in Arabic took up these numerals and spread them across a vast region, and from there they reached Europe, where merchants at first distrusted them because a zero could easily be changed into another digit. Within a few centuries, however, the convenience of the new system won out.

The passage is mainly concerned with:', '["why Roman numerals are better than modern ones", "how the use of zero developed and spread", "how merchants cheated with numbers", "the life of a famous mathematician"]', 1, 'It traces zero from Indian mathematics, through Arabic scholarship, to Europe.', '{"0": "The passage calls Roman numerals clumsy.", "2": "Fear of forgery is one detail.", "3": "No individual''s life is described."}'),
