  ('NEN-0036', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Main idea', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Read the passage and answer the question.
It is tempting to think of sleep as time lost, hours in which nothing useful happens. Research suggests the opposite. In a typical experiment, two groups learn a list of word pairs. One group then sleeps, while the other stays awake for the same length of time. When both are tested, the group that slept usually remembers more. Recordings of brain activity offer a clue: during deep sleep, patterns seen while learning appear to be replayed, as if the brain were rehearsing the day''s lessons. None of this means that one can learn a new language simply by sleeping with a recording playing. Sleep seems to strengthen what was learned while awake; it does not replace the learning itself.

The central claim of the passage is that:', '["people can learn languages while asleep", "sleep is time wasted", "sleep helps to strengthen memories formed while awake", "brain activity stops during deep sleep"]', 2, 'The experiment and the replay evidence support sleep''s role in strengthening learning.', '{"0": "The passage explicitly denies this.", "1": "That is the view the passage rejects.", "3": "Activity is replayed, not stopped."}'),
