  ('NEN-0041', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Main idea', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Read the passage and answer the question.
On a summer night, the centre of a large city can be several degrees warmer than the farmland around it. This ''urban heat island'' has simple causes. Roads, roofs and walls absorb sunlight during the day and release the stored heat slowly after dark. Trees and fields, by contrast, cool the air as water evaporates from their leaves and soil, and a city has far fewer of them. Engines and air conditioners add heat of their own. The effect is more than a matter of comfort: in a heatwave, people in the hottest districts, often the poorest, face real danger. Some cities now paint roofs white to reflect sunlight and plant trees along streets, measures that are cheap compared with the cost of illness.

The passage mainly explains:', '["why farmland is colder than forests", "how air conditioners work", "why cities should not have roads", "the causes and dangers of urban heat islands and some remedies"]', 3, 'It covers why cities are warmer, why that matters, and what cities do about it.', '{"0": "Forests are not compared with farmland.", "1": "Air conditioners are one cause.", "2": "Roads are a cause, but the passage does not argue against them."}'),
