  ('NEN-0046', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Character', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Read the passage and answer the question.
Every Friday my grandfather opened the back of the old wall clock and laid its parts on a white cloth. I was allowed to watch but not to touch. He worked without hurry, lifting each tiny wheel with tweezers, holding it to the light, and setting it down in exactly the place it had come from. Once I asked why he bothered, since the clock was never more than a minute slow. He did not answer at once. Then he said that a thing looked after is a thing that lasts, and that the clock had been his father''s. When he put the parts back and the ticking started again, he would sit for a moment with his eyes closed, listening, as if to a voice he knew.

