In the passage, ''bothered'' most nearly means:', '["annoyed someone", "took the trouble", "worried", "complained"]', 1, 'The child asks why he took the trouble when the clock kept good time.', '{"0": "That sense does not fit ''why he bothered''.", "2": "He was not worried.", "3": "He did not complain."}'),
  ('NEN-0051', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Sentence completion', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Choose the word or words that best complete the sentence.
The bridge was so ______ that engineers closed it until repairs could be made.', '["sturdy", "new", "unsafe", "popular"]', 2, 'Closing a bridge for repairs means it was unsafe.', '{"0": "A sturdy bridge would not need closing.", "1": "Newness gives no reason to close it.", "3": "Popularity is not a reason for repairs."}'),
  ('NEN-0052', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Sentence completion', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Choose the word or words that best complete the sentence.
Although the exam was long, the questions were ______, so most students finished on time.', '["confusing", "endless", "unfair", "straightforward"]', 3, '''Although the exam was long'' sets up a contrast: the questions were easy to answer.', '{"0": "Confusing questions would slow students down.", "1": "That repeats ''long'' instead of contrasting with it.", "2": "Unfairness does not explain finishing on time."}'),
  ('NEN-0053', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Sentence completion', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Choose the word or words that best complete the sentence.
The scientist was ______ about the early results, warning that the experiment needed to be repeated before anyone celebrated.', '["cautious", "ecstatic", "careless", "indifferent"]', 0, 'Warning against celebrating shows caution.', '{"1": "Ecstatic people celebrate.", "2": "Careless people do not warn about repeating tests.", "3": "Someone indifferent would not bother to warn."}'),
  ('NEN-0054', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Sentence completion', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Choose the word or words that best complete the sentence.
Far from being ______, the speech was so dull that half the audience fell asleep.', '["boring", "captivating", "short", "quiet"]', 1, '''Far from being X'' means it was the opposite of X; a dull speech is the opposite of captivating.', '{"0": "''Far from being boring'' contradicts ''so dull''.", "2": "Length is not contrasted with dullness.", "3": "Volume is not the contrast."}'),
  ('NEN-0055', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Sentence completion', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Choose the word or words that best complete the sentence.
The new medicine had only ______ side effects, such as a mild headache, so doctors prescribed it widely.', '["severe", "fatal", "minor", "permanent"]', 2, 'A mild headache is a minor side effect.', '{"0": "A mild headache is not severe.", "1": "Fatal effects would stop prescriptions.", "3": "A headache that passes is not permanent."}'),
  ('NEN-0056', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Sentence completion', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Choose the word or words that best complete the sentence.
Hina''s explanation was so ______ that even the youngest students understood it at once.', '["obscure", "lengthy", "hesitant", "lucid"]', 3, 'Lucid means clear, which is why everyone understood it.', '{"0": "Obscure means hard to understand.", "1": "Length does not make something easier.", "2": "A hesitant explanation is less clear."}'),
  ('NEN-0057', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Sentence completion', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Choose the word or words that best complete the sentence.
The village was so ______ that the nearest shop was a two-hour walk away.', '["crowded", "modern", "noisy", "remote"]', 3, 'Remote means far from other places.', '{"0": "Crowded villages tend to have shops nearby.", "1": "Modern does not imply distance.", "2": "Noise has nothing to do with distance."}'),
  ('NEN-0058', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Sentence completion', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Choose the word or words that best complete the sentence.
Instead of admitting his mistake, the manager tried to ______ the blame onto his assistant.', '["accept", "share", "shift", "forgive"]', 2, 'Avoiding admission means moving blame to someone else.', '{"0": "Accepting blame would be admitting the mistake.", "1": "Sharing blame still partly admits it.", "3": "One forgives a person, not blame onto someone."}'),
  ('NEN-0059', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Sentence completion', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Choose the word or words that best complete the sentence.
The critic''s review was ______: it praised nothing and found fault with every scene.', '["glowing", "scathing", "balanced", "vague"]', 1, 'A review that found fault everywhere is scathing, harshly critical.', '{"0": "Glowing means full of praise.", "2": "Balanced would mention some good points.", "3": "It was specific about every fault."}'),
  ('NEN-0060', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Sentence completion', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Choose the word or words that best complete the sentence.
The diplomat was known for being ______, choosing her words carefully so as not to offend either side.', '["tactful", "blunt", "arrogant", "talkative"]', 0, 'Choosing words to avoid offence is tactful.', '{"1": "Blunt speech risks offence.", "2": "Arrogance tends to offend.", "3": "Talking a lot is not the same as choosing words carefully."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, mdcat, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'English', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('NEN-0061', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Sentence completion', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Choose the word or words that best complete the sentence.
Because the evidence was only ______, the judge refused to convict without further proof.', '["conclusive", "circumstantial", "overwhelming", "undeniable"]', 1, 'Needing further proof means the evidence was indirect, i.e. circumstantial.', '{"0": "Conclusive evidence would settle the case.", "2": "Overwhelming evidence would lead to conviction.", "3": "Undeniable evidence needs no further proof."}'),
  ('NEN-0062', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Sentence completion', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Choose the word or words that best complete the sentence.
The professor''s lectures were ______, wandering from topic to topic with no clear thread.', '["rambling", "concise", "focused", "rehearsed"]', 0, 'Wandering with no thread is rambling.', '{"1": "Concise means brief and to the point.", "2": "Focused is the opposite of wandering.", "3": "Rehearsed lectures tend to follow a plan."}'),
  ('NEN-0063', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Sentence completion', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Choose the word or words that best complete the sentence.
Once a ______ town of a few hundred people, the place has grown into a busy city.', '["bustling", "vast", "crowded", "sleepy"]', 3, '''Once'' and ''has grown into a busy city'' contrast a quiet past with a busy present.', '{"0": "Bustling would not contrast with busy.", "1": "A few hundred people is not vast.", "2": "Crowded does not fit a few hundred people."}'),
  ('NEN-0064', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Sentence completion', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Choose the word or words that best complete the sentence.
The athlete''s ______ training paid off when she broke the national record.', '["occasional", "lazy", "rigorous", "brief"]', 2, 'Breaking a record suggests hard, rigorous training.', '{"0": "Occasional training is unlikely to break records.", "1": "Lazy contradicts the success.", "3": "Brief training is unlikely to pay off so well."}'),
  ('NEN-0065', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Sentence completion', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Choose the word or words that best complete the sentence.
The committee''s decision was ______; no one could change it, however hard they argued.', '["temporary", "irrevocable", "tentative", "flexible"]', 1, 'Irrevocable means it cannot be changed.', '{"0": "Temporary decisions end anyway.", "2": "Tentative means not yet final.", "3": "Flexible means open to change."}'),
  ('NEN-0066', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Sentence completion', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Choose the word or words that best complete the sentence.
The instructions were ______, so different readers understood them in different ways.', '["ambiguous", "precise", "printed", "short"]', 0, 'Being read in different ways means ambiguous.', '{"1": "Precise instructions have one meaning.", "2": "Being printed does not cause different readings.", "3": "Short instructions can still be clear."}'),
  ('NEN-0067', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Sentence completion', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Choose the word or words that best complete the sentence.
The old library, ______ for decades, was finally restored and reopened to the public.', '["admired", "crowded", "renovated", "neglected"]', 3, '''Finally restored'' implies it had been neglected.', '{"0": "Being admired does not explain the need for restoration.", "1": "A crowded library would not need reopening.", "2": "It is only now being restored."}'),
  ('NEN-0068', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Sentence completion', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Choose the word or words that best complete the sentence.
His ______ manner at the funeral surprised everyone; he joked and laughed as if at a party.', '["solemn", "grief-stricken", "flippant", "respectful"]', 2, 'Joking at a funeral is flippant, unsuitably light-hearted.', '{"0": "Solemn is the expected manner, so it would not surprise.", "1": "Grief would not show as joking.", "3": "Joking at a funeral is not respectful."}'),
  ('NEN-0069', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Sentence completion', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Choose the word or words that best complete the sentence.
The drought was so ______ that even the deepest wells ran dry.', '["severe", "mild", "brief", "local"]', 0, 'Wells running dry means a severe drought.', '{"1": "A mild drought would not empty the deepest wells.", "2": "A brief drought is unlikely to do this.", "3": "Extent in space is not the point."}'),
  ('NEN-0070', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Sentence completion', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Choose the word or words that best complete the sentence.
Critics called the policy ______, since it was meant to cut costs but in fact made everything more expensive.', '["economical", "effective", "counterproductive", "popular"]', 2, 'A measure that causes the opposite of its aim is counterproductive.', '{"0": "It made things more expensive, not cheaper.", "1": "It failed at its aim.", "3": "Popularity is not discussed."}'),
  ('NEN-0071', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Sentence completion', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Choose the word or words that best complete the sentence.
The children were ______ to start the trip, waking up before dawn and packing their bags twice.', '["reluctant", "eager", "afraid", "too tired"]', 1, 'Waking early and packing twice shows eagerness.', '{"0": "Reluctant children would not wake early.", "2": "Fear does not fit the excitement shown.", "3": "Their energy contradicts tiredness."}'),
  ('NEN-0072', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Sentence completion', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Choose the word or words that best complete the sentence.
The author''s style is ______: she says in ten words what others need fifty to say.', '["verbose", "ornate", "repetitive", "succinct"]', 3, 'Saying much in few words is succinct.', '{"0": "Verbose means using too many words.", "1": "Ornate means elaborate, not brief.", "2": "Repetition adds words."}'),
  ('NEN-0073', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Sentence completion', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Choose the word or words that best complete the sentence.
The detective remained ______ about the suspect''s story, pointing out that the times did not match.', '["convinced", "certain", "pleased", "doubtful"]', 3, 'Pointing out a mismatch shows doubt.', '{"0": "Convinced means believing it.", "1": "Certain of the story''s truth contradicts the doubt.", "2": "Pleasure does not fit finding a flaw."}'),
  ('NEN-0074', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Sentence completion', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Choose the word or words that best complete the sentence.
Though generally ______, the minister could be surprisingly stubborn when his principles were at stake.', '["inflexible", "accommodating", "obstinate", "rigid"]', 1, '''Though generally'' followed by ''surprisingly stubborn'' needs a word to be the opposite of stubborn.', '{"0": "Inflexible is not a contrast with stubborn.", "2": "Obstinate means stubborn.", "3": "Rigid means stubborn too."}'),
  ('NEN-0075', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Sentence completion', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Choose the word or words that best complete the sentence.
The writer''s early novels were ______, but her later work won wide acclaim.', '["overlooked", "celebrated", "translated", "admired"]', 0, '''But her later work won wide acclaim'' contrasts with early work that was ignored.', '{"1": "Celebrated would not contrast with acclaim.", "2": "Translation is not the opposite of acclaim.", "3": "Admired matches acclaim instead of contrasting."}'),
  ('NEN-0076', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Sentence completion', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Choose the word or words that best complete the sentence.
The rescue team worked ______ through the night, refusing to rest until every survivor was found.', '["lazily", "carelessly", "tirelessly", "occasionally"]', 2, 'Refusing to rest means working tirelessly.', '{"0": "That contradicts refusing to rest.", "1": "Care is not the point; effort is.", "3": "They worked without stopping."}'),
  ('NEN-0077', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Sentence completion', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Choose the word or words that best complete the sentence.
To ______ the spread of the disease, schools were closed for two weeks.', '["encourage", "curb", "measure", "announce"]', 1, 'Closing schools is meant to curb, or limit, spread.', '{"0": "Closing schools would not be meant to encourage spread.", "2": "Closing schools does not measure anything.", "3": "Announcing spread is not the aim of closing schools."}'),
  ('NEN-0078', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Sentence completion', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Choose the word or words that best complete the sentence.
The data were so ______ that the researchers could draw no firm conclusion from them.', '["inconsistent", "reliable", "complete", "clear-cut"]', 0, 'Data that allow no firm conclusion are inconsistent.', '{"1": "Reliable data would support conclusions.", "2": "Complete data would help.", "3": "Clear-cut data allow a conclusion."}'),
  ('NEN-0079', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Sentence completion (two blanks)', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Choose the word or words that best complete the sentence.
Sentence completion (two blanks): The trail was ______ at first, but it soon became so ______ that we had to climb on hands and knees.', '["steep / gentle", "steep / flat", "gentle / level", "gentle / steep"]', 3, '''At first'' and ''but soon'' mark a change, and climbing on hands and knees needs a steep slope.', '{"0": "Reversed: a gentle slope needs no climbing on hands and knees.", "1": "A flat trail needs no climbing.", "2": "A level trail needs no climbing."}'),
  ('NEN-0080', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Sentence completion (two blanks)', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Choose the word or words that best complete the sentence.
Sentence completion (two blanks): Although the plan seemed ______ on paper, it proved ______ in practice.', '["sound / successful", "flawed / useless", "sound / unworkable", "weak / poor"]', 2, '''Although'' needs a contrast: good on paper, bad in practice.', '{"0": "No contrast: both are positive.", "1": "No contrast: both are negative.", "3": "No contrast: both are negative."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, mdcat, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'English', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('NEN-0081', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Sentence completion (two blanks)', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Choose the word or words that best complete the sentence.
Sentence completion (two blanks): The professor was ______ by the student''s answer, which showed a ______ understanding far beyond his years.', '["impressed / sophisticated", "disappointed / shallow", "impressed / shallow", "bored / deep"]', 0, 'Understanding ''far beyond his years'' is sophisticated, and it would impress.', '{"1": "A shallow answer is not ''far beyond his years''.", "2": "One is not impressed by shallowness.", "3": "A deep answer would not bore."}'),
  ('NEN-0082', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Sentence completion (two blanks)', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Choose the word or words that best complete the sentence.
Sentence completion (two blanks): Far from ______ the dispute, the new rules only ______ it.', '["resolving / settled", "resolving / worsened", "worsening / inflamed", "settling / ended"]', 1, '''Far from'' with ''only'' needs opposites: they did not resolve it but worsened it.', '{"0": "Resolving and settling mean the same.", "2": "Both blanks mean making it worse.", "3": "Settling and ending mean the same."}'),
  ('NEN-0083', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Sentence completion (two blanks)', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Choose the word or words that best complete the sentence.
Sentence completion (two blanks): Initially ______ the new technology, farmers became ______ once they saw their harvests double.', '["excited by / doubtful", "wary of / suspicious", "wary of / enthusiastic", "keen on / eager"]', 2, '''Initially'' and ''once they saw'' show a change from doubt to enthusiasm after success.', '{"0": "Doubling the harvest would not make them doubtful.", "1": "Both show doubt; no change.", "3": "Both show keenness; no change."}'),
  ('NEN-0084', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Sentence completion (two blanks)', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Choose the word or words that best complete the sentence.
Sentence completion (two blanks): The medicine works ______, so patients feel better within minutes, but its effect is ______ and wears off in an hour.', '["slowly / lasting", "quickly / permanent", "slowly / brief", "quickly / short-lived"]', 3, 'Within minutes is quick; wearing off in an hour is short-lived.', '{"0": "Within minutes is not slow.", "1": "Wearing off is not permanent.", "2": "Within minutes is not slow."}'),
  ('NEN-0085', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Sentence completion (two blanks)', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Choose the word or words that best complete the sentence.
Sentence completion (two blanks): Her argument was ______ but not ______: it was well organised, yet it rested on false facts.', '["accurate / coherent", "coherent / accurate", "confused / true", "convincing / organised"]', 1, 'Well organised means coherent; resting on false facts means not accurate.', '{"0": "Reversed: it was organised but not accurate.", "2": "It was well organised, so not confused.", "3": "It was organised, so ''not organised'' contradicts the sentence."}'),
  ('NEN-0086', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Sentence completion (two blanks)', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Choose the word or words that best complete the sentence.
Sentence completion (two blanks): The once ______ river had become so ______ that no fish could survive in it.', '["polluted / clean", "dirty / toxic", "clear / pure", "pristine / polluted"]', 3, '''Once'' and ''had become'' signal a change from clean to polluted.', '{"0": "A clean river is where fish would survive.", "1": "No change: both are bad.", "2": "No change, and fish would survive."}'),
  ('NEN-0087', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Sentence completion (two blanks)', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Choose the word or words that best complete the sentence.
Sentence completion (two blanks): The speaker''s voice was so ______ that the people at the back, straining to hear, began to ______.', '["faint / complain", "loud / complain", "faint / cheer", "clear / leave"]', 0, 'Straining to hear means a faint voice, which would lead to complaints.', '{"1": "A loud voice needs no straining.", "2": "Straining to hear is not a reason to cheer.", "3": "A clear voice needs no straining."}'),
  ('NEN-0088', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Sentence completion (two blanks)', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Choose the word or words that best complete the sentence.
Sentence completion (two blanks): Because the evidence was ______, the scientists'' conclusions were necessarily ______.', '["plentiful / tentative", "limited / certain", "limited / tentative", "convincing / doubtful"]', 2, 'Limited evidence leads to tentative, cautious conclusions.', '{"0": "Plentiful evidence would allow firmer conclusions.", "1": "Limited evidence cannot give certainty.", "3": "Convincing evidence would not make conclusions doubtful."}'),
  ('NEN-0089', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Sentence completion (two blanks)', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Choose the word or words that best complete the sentence.
Sentence completion (two blanks): Some readers found the novel ______, while others, who disliked its slow pace, thought it ______.', '["tedious / dull", "absorbing / tedious", "absorbing / gripping", "short / long"]', 1, '''While others, who disliked'' contrasts the first view: absorbing versus tedious.', '{"0": "Both are negative; no contrast.", "2": "Both are positive, but the others disliked it.", "3": "Pace is not length."}'),
  ('NEN-0090', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Sentence completion (two blanks)', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Choose the word or words that best complete the sentence.
Sentence completion (two blanks): The team''s victory was ______, since they had been ______ by everyone to lose badly.', '["unexpected / predicted", "expected / predicted", "unexpected / encouraged", "easy / warned"]', 0, 'If everyone predicted a heavy loss, the win was unexpected.', '{"1": "A predicted loss makes victory unexpected, not expected.", "2": "One is not encouraged to lose.", "3": "One is not warned to lose, and the win was not easy."}'),
  ('NEN-0091', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Sentence completion (two blanks)', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Choose the word or words that best complete the sentence.
Sentence completion (two blanks): The minister''s speech was ______ rather than ______: it stirred feelings but offered few facts.', '["informative / emotional", "factual / moving", "dull / lively", "emotional / informative"]', 3, 'Stirring feelings is emotional; few facts means not informative.', '{"0": "Reversed: it had feelings, not facts.", "1": "Few facts means not factual.", "2": "Stirring feelings is not dull."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

